+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+The record store must decide how two submissions derived from the same visit become the current record. Field workers edit offline for up to 48 hours and independently correct the same visit after comparing notes, so concurrent edits are expected rather than exceptional. The HLD sets the stakes: the server must not treat arrival order as authority, must not silently discard a submitted edit, must keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable, and must keep workflows operable by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator.
+
+This decision sits below the site-catalog contract and above the tablet-sync and review-export designs, which are sized around whichever policy it selects. It extends the record-store design at `docs/intent/record-store/record-store-design.md`.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates either contender: both can retain original submissions, and both can be made inspectable. The choice therefore turns on weighted criteria.
+
+- **No unsignaled combination of related edits** (major; turns on the *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* tenet and the non-goal against inferring scientific intent). The server cannot infer which fields are scientifically related — a species identification and its cover estimate being the motivating example. A policy that combines fields automatically can produce a value no worker submitted, without a person noticing.
+- **Inspectability of an exported value** (major; turns on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). A coordinator must be able to trace an exported value back to submitted observations.
+- **Coordinator review load** (major). How much unresolved work reaches a coordinator, and how much stays out of the Friday export.
+- **Operational burden for two coordinators** (moderate; turns on *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). Rules that require maintenance are a recurring operational cost.
+- **Offline submission cost** (minor). What each tablet stores and sends per edit over intermittent links.
+- **Implementation effort at the record store** (moderate). How much server logic each policy requires.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission is a complete visit that names the revision it was derived from — its base revision. The server records the submission as a new revision with a server-assigned ID, author, and receipt time. A revision whose base is the visit's current revision advances the visit; a revision whose base is stale is a competing revision. Competing revisions are kept unmerged and handed to a coordinator, who designates one as the resolved current revision. All original submissions are retained, resolved or not.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two tablets fetch visit V at revision R. Tablet A corrects an observer name; tablet B adds a missing species. Both submit against base R. A's revision advances V to its next revision; B's revision sees a base that no longer matches the current revision, becomes competing, and V enters the review queue. A coordinator designates B (or A) as resolved; both submissions remain in V's history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No unsignaled combination of related edits: **strong** — the server never combines fields; every resolved value is one worker's submitted visit.
+- Inspectability: **strong** — each exported value maps to a single revision and its author.
+- Coordinator review load: **weak** — even edits to unrelated fields become review work, and unresolved visits stay out of the export.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — no merge rules to maintain.
+- Offline submission cost: **weak** — each edit sends the full visit rather than a diff.
+- Implementation effort: **partial** — little merge logic, but revision lineage, conflict detection, and the resolution operation are all required.
+
+**Summary.** Preserves each visit as an indivisible scientific unit, at the cost of a larger review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base state plus structured field changes. The server applies a three-way merge: changes touching disjoint fields combine automatically, while changes to the same field — or to fields the rules treat as coupled — go to review. Rules exist per field kind: scalars (last-writer or review), observer lists (set merge), and species entries (keyed merge), maintained as the data model evolves. Original submissions are retained and combined fields carry field-level provenance.
+
+**Demonstration.** From base R, A corrects an observer name and B adds a species. The rules see disjoint fields and combine them into a resolved current state with no review. If A and B both revise the same species' cover estimate, the rules flag an overlap and send it to review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No unsignaled combination of related edits: **weak** — the rules combine scientifically related edits they do not recognize as coupled; the server cannot infer the species/cover relationship, so a wrong combination can land without a person noticing.
+- Inspectability: **partial** — field-level provenance traces individual values, but an exported field may be an automatic composition rather than one worker's submitted visit.
+- Coordinator review load: **strong** — disjoint edits resolve themselves and more visits are export-ready.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the rule set must be maintained and kept correct as fields and species data evolve.
+- Offline submission cost: **strong** — tablets send base state plus structured changes rather than whole visits.
+- Implementation effort: **weak** — three-way merge plus maintained per-field-kind rules.
+
+**Summary.** Reduces review volume and submission size, at the cost of maintained rules that can silently combine related edits.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** The two major criteria grounded in the project's own intent — never combining related edits without a person noticing, and mapping an exported value to a submitted observation — dominate the criteria that field-aware merge wins (review load and submission size). Field-aware merge does not discard data, but it can combine a species identification from one worker with a cover estimate from another under rules that do not understand their scientific relationship, producing a submitted-looking value no one submitted. Whole-visit revisions never do this, because the server never combines fields at all. Between these, the project accepts a larger review queue over rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing.
+
+Field-aware merge remains credible and would become the better choice if coordinator review load or submission size became the binding constraint, or if the domain's field relationships could be made machine-representable enough for the rules to honor them; the decision is revisited if any of those changes.
+
+Committing to whole-visit revisions forecloses automatic combination and the smaller submissions it allows. It obligates downstream work:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain each visit's base revision ID, submit complete-visit revisions under an idempotency key, size local storage for full visit payloads, and display pending competing revisions and server outcomes to field workers.
+- **Review-export** must provide a whole-revision comparison view for a visit's competing revisions and hold unresolved visits out of the export set; only resolved visits are export-eligible.
+
+Both obligations are cascades. The decision's substance is the record store's concurrency policy and the revision/resolution contract it implies; tablet-sync and review-export consume that contract and are designed around it from their own nodes.
+
+The decision turns on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*, reinforced by the non-goal against inferring scientific intent.
